"The Shapley allocation of a graph energy game can leave its core | trureturing (https://www.trureturing.org/research/aa2012/)\nciteturn0search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 days ago; # The Shapley allocation of a graph energy game can leave its core ... The explicit vertex labels $0,1$ in `Fin 52` choose a counterexample; they add no hypothesis to the source claim.\n\n# The Shapley allocation of a graph energy game can leave its core\n\nLiterature status: not rechecked Repository record: refuted\n\nResolution source: D5/S3/Combinatorics/SpectralGraph/ArizmendiEnergyGameShapleyRefutation.result10.1016/j.dam.2023.01.030Source dossierRelease 36a49830636b\n\nProblem\n\n## The question\n\nGerardo Arizmendi and Octavio Arizmendi, The graph energy game, arXiv:2012.14114v2, Conjecture 3.10, page 8:\n\n> For the energy game, the Shapley value is in the core.\n\nThe source defines the energy of a finite simple undirected graph as the sum of absolute adjacency eigenvalues, with multiplicity. Its game has $w_G(S)=\\mathcal E(G[S])$. For each vertex $i$ its allocation is\n\n$$ \\phi_i(w_G)=\\sum_{S\\subseteq V\\setminus{i}} \\frac{|S|!(|V|-|S|-1)!}{|V|!} \\bigl(w_G(S\\cup{i})-w_G(S)\\bigr). $$\n\nThe core consists of all $x$ satisfying $\\sum_{i\\in S}x_i\\ge w_G(S)$ for every coalition $S$ and $\\sum_{i\\in V}x_i=w_G(V)$. The conjecture quantifies over every finite simple graph, without connectedness, degree or bipartiteness assumptions. Preregistration #21951 binds this universal proposition and its expected refutation. The Library note records the versioned source and the literal defining formulas.\n\n### Statement echo\n\nAll local names below belong to `D5.S3.Combinatorics.SpectralGraph.ArizmendiEnergyGameShapleyRefutation`.\n\n| Source clause | Lean counterpart | Fidelity | | --- | --- | --- | | Finite simple undirected graph, every size | `claim`, `n : Nat`, `G : SimpleGraph (Fin n)` | Includes size zero; no extra graph hypothesis | | Energy of the induced coalition | `worth` | Reuses `CyclicExtensionRefutation.graphEnergy` with `MatrixKind.adjacency`, whose center is zero | | Every subset of the other players | `shapley`, powerset of `univ.erase i` | Includes empty and full predecessor sets | | Factorial coefficient and marginal contribution | `shapley` | Natural factorials and subtraction, followed by real coercions and real division | | Coalition payoff total | Finite sum in `core` | Inlined sum, with no duplicate payoff-total definition | | Every coalition inequality and efficiency | The two conjuncts of `core` | Singleton, empty and grand coalitions included | | Universal core membership | `claim` | Literal source conclusion | | Refutation | `result` | Negates the entire universal claim |\n\nThe explicit vertex labels $0,1$ in `Fin 52` choose a counterexample; they add no hypothesis to the source claim. The private parameter $m\\ge50$ restricts a sufficient counterexample family, not the quantified claim.\n\n## Our foothold\n\nA scarce-side vertex of $K_{2,m}$ has a large average marginal contribution over predecessor coalitions. For $m\\ge50$ this average exceeds its marginal contribution to the grand coalition. Efficiency then conflicts with the lower payoff bound of the coalition excluding that vertex.\n\n## Missing bridges\n\nThe settlement is `Refuted`: `result` proves the negation of `claim`. All auxiliary theorems are private and used by the refutation. Their `bind-only` classification supplies no independent escape witness; the module's admission basis is `open-problem-resolution (#21951; Refuted)`. No atom or cover is involved.\n\nRoute\n\n## Proposed approach\n\nFor a coalition with $r$ vertices on the two-vertex side and $k$ vertices on the other side, its energy is $2\\sqrt{rk}$. The proof obtains this from the adjacency matrix identities $A^3=rkA$ and $\\operatorname{tr}(A^2)=2rk$. The existing frozen declaration `D5.S3.Arith.Lattices.Klartag.trace_mul_self_eq_sum_sq` supplies the Hermitian trace-square identity used by `energy_of_cubic`.\n\nWriting $q=\\sqrt2$, $H=(m+1)(m+2)$, $S_1=\\sum_{k=0}^m\\sqrt k$ and $S_3=\\sum_{k=0}^m k\\sqrt k$, `scarce_payoff` gives\n\n$$ \\phi_a=\\frac{2((m+q)S_1+(q-2)S_3)}{H}. $$\n\nThe private sum bounds give $S_1\\ge\\frac23m\\sqrt m$ and $S_3\\le\\frac25m^2\\sqrt m+m\\sqrt m$. The sign $q-2<0$ requires the upper bound on $S_3$. `scarce_formula_gap` establishes $\\phi_a>2(\\sqrt2-1)\\sqrt m$ for every $m\\ge50$. `family_not_core` combines this inequality with the efficiency conjunct and the coalition inequality at $V\\setminus{a}$ to derive a contradiction. `result` specializes to $m=50$.\n\nFalsifier\n\n## What would falsify this route\n\nThe source claim would require both efficiency and every coalition inequality on $K_{2,50}$. The contradiction in `family_not_core` rules out that conjunction. No separately formalized universal efficiency theorem is asserted. An exact prior literature settlement would remove the novelty basis; the literature screen below is explicitly incomplete.\n\n## Evidence to collect\n\nThe Lean source and its full-statement Blueprint are D5 and Blueprint. The axiom closure of every public declaration is contained in ${\\texttt{propext},\\texttt{Classical.choice},\\texttt{Quot.sound}}$. The public result is the designated refutation result (`basis=refutes`) and is exempt from four-slot escape registration (CLAUDE.md §3.9). There are no other source-authored public theorems or published owners. Compiler-generated congruence companions are machine companion names under CLAUDE.md §3.9.\n\nExperiment: exact checker at immutable commit 482b7177598d82c15ee82daca692752e471ee567. The committed `check.py` was extracted without modification and run as `python3 /Users/auric/.sshx/327280fbd116be4105813297/attempt-1/experiment-check.py` (exit 0, `ALL_CHECKS_OK`). Its SHA-256 is `5e2feaa724951623c0a3e8730b1a9727c71f70ab2d6f434869d5a9fdccc20125`. The script uses exact integer, rational and symbolic arithmetic. It checks all eight coalition constraints of $P_3$; 30 characteristic polynomials for $0\\le r\\le4$, $0\\le k\\le5$; full subset Shapley formulas for $1\\le m\\le4$; factorial identities and normalization for $0\\le m\\le70$; positive deficit enclosures at $m=50,64,100$; and the actual adjacency matrix of $K_{2,50}$. Its symbolic leading-coefficient calculation is separate from a uniform Lean asymptotic theorem.\n\nTriage\n\n## Scope assessment\n\n  1. Proved in Lean: every $K_{2,m}$ with $m\\ge50$ fails Shapley core membership. The scarce-side payoff exceeds $2(\\sqrt2-1)\\sqrt m$. Evidence: private `scarce_formula_gap` and `family_not_core`, consumed by public `result`.\n  2. Proved in Lean: for that same actual family graph, assumed core membership supplies its own efficiency equality and the inequality for $V\\setminus{a}$; the scarce-side overshoot contradicts them. Evidence: the concluding contradiction in `family_not_core`.\n  3. Computed: $K_{2,50}$ is connected, has minimum degree 2, and has blocking deficit in the exact interval $[1380588425475056173/6630000000000000000, 1380588425475064157/6630000000000000000]$, whose lower endpoint is positive. Evidence: the pinned checker, command, exit code and SHA-256 in Evidence; these graph properties and this exact interval are not separately stated as Lean theorems.\n  4. Computed: $P_3$ retains Example 3.9's allocation, center first, $((2+2\\sqrt2)/3,(2\\sqrt2-1)/3,(2\\sqrt2-1)/3)$, and passes all eight core constraints. Evidence: the same pinned checker and exact eight-coalition calculation in Evidence.\n  5. Open: no Lean theorem of unbounded absolute blocking deficit or its asymptotic limit is delivered. The checker computes the symbolic candidate coefficient $(26-18\\sqrt2)/15$ and its positive sign; that calculation does not certify convergence in Lean.\n  6. Open: the optimal threshold in $m$ and classifications for all trees, regular graphs or balanced complete bipartite graphs remain unsettled by these results.\n  7. Proved in the published paper, not in this Lean module: the different vertex-energy allocation belongs to the core. Evidence: independently read arXiv v2, Theorem 3.5 (the induced-subgraph energy inequality) and Theorem 3.6 (core membership). The source labels 3.6 a theorem. This allocation is not the refuted Shapley allocation.\n\n### What the settlement shows\n\nThe proved mechanism is an imbalance between a scarce-side vertex's average marginal contribution and its grand-coalition marginal contribution. `scarce_formula_gap` and `family_not_core` show that connectedness and minimum degree 2 do not rescue the universal conjecture; the two graph properties of the concrete witness are computed as item 3. The sufficient bound $m\\ge50$ is proved, without an optimality claim.\n\nThe nearest verified surviving example here is $P_3$, computed in item 4. The source's vertex-energy allocation theorem concerns a different allocation and survives this refutation. Proposition 3.11's marginal lower bound and Proposition 4.13's assertion that the 2-energy Shapley value equals degree are separate published statements; neither uses Conjecture 3.10 in its proof. They are literature readings, not new Lean results. No additional source conclusion is invalidated by this delivery.\n\nASSUMED-UNVERIFIED\n\n## Unverified assumptions\n\nLiterature completeness is `ASSUMED-UNVERIFIED`. The preregistration's bounded screen found no exact settlement in the retrieved texts. It includes the versioned source, cited and same-author papers, broad bibliographic and arXiv searches, and MathOverflow/MathDB readings. The journal full text (HTTP 403), Rather–Ganie–Imran, Iñarra–Usategui, Vertex energy of some integral graphs, API limits and the partial broad full-text search leave explicit unchecked scope. The spectral reduction, Shapley formula and sum-comparison method are literature-attested ingredients. The family calculation and blocking conclusion are `suspected-novel` within that searched scope only.-