bution, which we prove is in the core. These are the main results of the article.\n\nTHE GRAPH ENERGY GAME 5\n\nThe energy of a vertex, thought as a value restricted the energy game on graphs, also satisﬁes\n\nthe axioms of null player, symmetry and eﬃciency. However, it is easy to see that the Shapley value\n\ndoes not always coincide with the energy of a vertex.\n\n3.1. The energy game. We now deﬁne the object of our interest, namely, the energy game on\n\ngraphs.\n\nDeﬁnition 3.1. Let G= (V , E) be a simple undirected graph. For each subset S⊂V let w(S) :=\n\nE(I(S)) where I(S) is the graph induced by S. We denote (V , w), the energy game on G.\n\nThe following theorem justiﬁes the interest in the deﬁnition above.\n\nProposition 3.2. For any graph G= (V, E ), the game (V, w)is superadditive.\n\nProof. The proposition is equivalent to proving that if the adjacency matrix of a graph G is parti-\n\ntioned as\n\nA(G) = X Y\n\nZ W ,\n\nwith X and W square matrices of dimension k and n−k, respectively, then T r(|X|) + T r(|W|)≤\n\nT r(|A(G)|). This may be proved by the use of Lemma 4.17 of [9] (see also Theorem 3 in [12]).\n\nWe need to prove that for each S⊂V,w E(S)≥e(S) that is\n\nE(I(S)) ≤X\n\ni∈S E(v i)\n\nwhere E(v i) is the vertex energy of v i in G and E(G(S)) is the energy of of the graph induced by\n\nS. The result now follows from Theorem 3.5. \n\nRemark: Theorem 3.5 gives an inequality useful for bounding the energy of a graph. For\n\nexample, for two connected vertices v and w the theorem implies that E(v) + E(w)≥2, this has\n\nbeen proved independently in [1]. Another example is given by Theorem 4.20 in [9] for which we\n\ngive an alternative proof.\n\nTheorem 3.7. If F is an edge cut of a simple graph, then E(G−F)≤ E(G).\n\nTHE GRAPH ENERGY GAME 7\n\nProof. Since F is an edge cut of G,G−F=H∪K, where H and K are two complementary\n\ninduced graphs. We can split the set vertices of G as V H∪V K where the V H is the set of vertices\n\nof H and V K is the set of vertices in H. By Theorem 3.5,\n\nE(H)≤X\n\nv∈V H E(v),and\n\nE(K)≤X\n\nv∈V K\n\n,E(v).\n\nHence,\n\nE(G−F) = E(H) + E(K)≤X\n\nv∈V H E(v) + X\n\nv∈V K E(v) = X\n\nv∈V(G)E(v) = E(G)\n\n\n\nIf one considers the energy game on graphs with n vertices, then the energy of a vertex can be\n\nthought as a value in these games. In this sense, one can ask if this value coincides with the Shapley\n\nvalue restricted to the energy game on graphs. The next theorem goes in this direction\n\nTheorem 3.8. The energy of a vertex satisﬁes the axioms of symmetry, eﬃciency and null player\n\n\nmust also have energy 2. This, of course, means that w is connected to v j in G. Since this works for\n\nall w∈N(v j), then N(v j)⊂N(v i). Interchanging the roles of v i and v j, we see that N(v i)⊂N(v j)\n\nand thus N(v i) = N(v j). \n\nEven though the energy of a vertex satisﬁes the above axioms of symmetry, eﬃciency and null\n\nplayer, it does not coincide with the Shapley value, as the following example shows.\n\n8 GERARDO ARIZMENDI AND OCTAVIO ARIZMENDI\n\nExample 3.9. Consider G= (V , E), with V={1,2,3}and E={{1,2},{2,3}}, i.e. G is the path\n\nwith 3 vertices. A direct calculation shows that E(v 1) = E(v 3) = 1\n\n√2 and E(v 2) = √2. On the other\n\nhand, the values of each coalition are given by\n\nE({}) = E({1}) = E({2}) = E({3}) = E({1,3})=0,\n\nE({1,2}) = E({2,3}) = 2,\n\nE({1,2,3})=2√2.\n\nHence, if we denote by ϕ(v i) the Shapley value of the i-th vertex then a direct calculation shows\n\nthat ϕ(v 1) = ϕ(v 3) = 2√2−1\n\n3 and ϕ(v 2) = 2+2√2\n\n3.\n\nHowever, we conjecture that the Shapley value is in the core.\n\nConjecture 3.10. For the energy game, the Shapley value is in the core.\n\nLet us end with the following lemma which says that the marginal contribution of a vertex in\n\na coalition is larger than the sum of the vertex energies of the coalition, which is a step in that\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Shapley value is in the core of the energy game — MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/349224/the-shapley-value-is-in-the-core-of-the-energy-game)\nciteturn0search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; Let $G=(V,E)$G=(V,E) be a graph, with the energy game assigning each coalition its graph energy and with $\\boldsymbol{\\varphi}$φ denoting the Shapley value.\n\nThe Shapley value is in the core of the energy game\n\nConjecture open\n\nLet $G=(V,E)$G=(V,E) be a graph, with the energy game assigning each coalition its graph energy and with $\\boldsymbol{\\varphi}$φ denoting the Shapley value. Energy-game core conjecture. For the energy game, the Shapley value is in the core. This conjecture asks whether the Shapley allocation satisfies all coalition inequalities for graph energy; the source gives an example showing that the energy value itself need not coincide with the Shapley value, but provides no resolution of the core-membership question.\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh\n\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.\n\nsource: Gerardo Arizmendi and Octavio Arizmendi, “The graph energy game”, arXiv:2012.14114 (2021).\n\ncooperative-game-theorygraph-energygraph-theory\n\n0\n\nposted by arXiv 5 days ago\n\n\n## 0 Replies\n\n* * *\n\nSign in to reply.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe graph energy game (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0166218X23000380)\nciteturn0search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.5 years ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; ### Efficient computation of the Shapley value for centrality in networks ... Taking into account the developed theory and in search of an interpretation that would obey intuition and allow us to understand the interaction between local and global energy in graphs, in [2] the authors defined the graph energy game.\n\n## Discrete Applied Mathematics\n\nVolume 330, 15 May 2023, Pages 128-140\n\n# The graph energy game\n\nDedicated to our father, Manuel Enrique Arizmendi San Pedro, on the occasion of his 65th birthday.\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1016/j.dam.2023.01.030Get rights and content\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe study the graph energy from a cooperative game viewpoint. We introduce the graph energy game and show several of its game-theoretic properties. In particular, we see that it is a superadditive game and that the vector of vertex energies belongs to the core of the game. These properties imply new bounds for the energy of graphs. We also consider a version based on $p$-Schatten norms.\n\n## Introduction\n\nIn this work, we propose to study Gutman’s graph energy [8] from the viewpoint of cooperative game theory. For this, we define a cooperative game based on the graph energy and study its main properties and solutions.\n\nCooperative game theory is a branch of game theory which models scenarios where different players may gain a larger utility by cooperating with each other. The worth of $S$ is given by the energy of the subgraph $H$, $\\mathcal{E}\\left(H\\right)$. We prove that this is a superadditive game.\n\nWe would also be interested in a particular payoff: the vector of vertex energies. The energy of a vertex as defined in [4] is a way to split the total energy of the graphs among the different vertices. In principle, it is natural to try to give an interpretation in terms of centrality, similar to the centrality measures studied in networks, such as degree, PageRank, betweenness, closeness, etc. However, from the observation of examples, we believe that one is led to the intuition that the vector of vertex energies is closer to a payoff in a cooperative game. Thus, in the above described game we consider the payment given by the vertex energy and prove that it actually satisfies the axioms of symmetry, null player and efficiency. More important we show that this payment is in the core and thus provide evidence for the above intuition (see Section 2.3 for definitions). This is the main result of the paper.\n\nWe also consider generalizations where we consider instead of the energy, a new family of “energies” based on the $p$-Schatten norms. Similar results are proved for these generalizations, obtaining new inequalities.\n\nWe hope that this paper motivates the study of graphs and their properties from the viewpoint of game theory.\n\nApart from this introduction, the paper contains five sections. In Section 2 we present the necessary preliminaries. 2.1 and 2.2 are devoted to graphs and energy of graph and vertices, 2.3 is concerned with matrix inequalities and in 2.4 we describe basic concepts of cooperative game theory. In Section 3, we prove the main results of the paper. We introduce a cooperative game based on the energy of a graph in 3.1. In 3.2 we consider the vertex energy from this viewpoint. In Section 4 we generalize these ideas for $p$-Schatten norms by introducing the $p$-Schatten energy. We start with basic properties of this energy in 4.1, then introduce the $p$-energy of a vertex in 4.2, and finally we specialize to the case $p=2$ in Section 4.3, proving that this case coincides with previously studied games. In Section 5, we present some examples. We finish with a small section regarding conclusions and further questions.\n\nWe address natural questions about this game. First, we prove that for each graph, the energy game is a superadditive game, although it is not always convex. That the game is superadditive implies non-trivial inequalities about the energies of graphs. In particular, one deduces that given a graph $G$, any induced subgraph of $G$ has less energy than the total graph. While this may seem obvious, there may\n\n## Schatten energy game\n\nRecalling that the energy of a graph is the 1-Schatten norm (or nuclear norm) of the adjacency matrix, in this section we want to consider an energy inspired by the $p-$Schatten norms.\n\nExplicitly, instead of using $\\left|A\\right|$ one can use ${\\left|A\\right|}^{p}$. In this way, the $p$-energy of a graph is defined as ${\\mathcal{E}}_{p}\\left(G\\right)≔tr\\left({\\left|A\\left(G\\right)\\right|}^{p}\\right)$ where $A\\left(G\\right)$ denotes the adjacency matrix of $G$.\n\nHere, we should point out that using the p$p$-Schatten norm, {\\left(tr\\left({\\left|A\\left(G\\right)\\right|}^{p}\\right)\\right)}^{1/p}${\\left(tr\\left({\\left|A\\left(G\\right)\\right|}^{p}\\right)\\right)}^{1/p}$, directly would result in a theory which is not superadditive.\n\nThe p$p$-energy\n\n\n(2020)\n\n  * Arizmendi O. et al.\n\n### On bounds for the energy of graphs and digraphs\n\n  * Chalkiadakis G. et al.\n\nThere are more references available in the full text version of this article.\n\n## Cited by (6)\n\n  * ### On the positive and negative p-energies of graphs under edge addition\n\n2026, Discrete Applied Mathematics\n\nShow abstract\n\nIn this paper, we introduce the concepts of positive and negative p$p$-energies of graphs and investigate their behavior under edge addition. Specifically, we generalize the classical notions of positive and negative square energies to the p$p$-energy setting, denoted by {\\mathcal{E}}_{p}^{+}\\left(G\\right)${\\mathcal{E}}_{p}^{+}\\left(G\\right)$ and {\\mathcal{E}}_{p}^{-}\\left(G\\right)${\\mathcal{E}}_{p}^{-}\\left(G\\right)$, respectively. We establish improved lower bounds for these quantities under edge addition, which sharpen existing results by Abiad et al. in the case p=2$p=2$. Furthermore, we address the monotonicity problem for {\\mathcal{E}}_{p}^{+}\\left(G\\right)${\\mathcal{E}}_{p}^{+}\\left(G\\right)$ under edge addition, and construct a family of counterexamples showing that monotonicity fails for 1\\le p<3$1\\le p<3$. In particular, the case p=0$p=0$ yields the inertia bound where {n}^{+}${n}^{+}$ and {n}^{-}${n}^{-}$ denote the number of positive and negative eigenvalues of {A}_{G}${A}_{G}$, respectively. This resolves two conjectures of Elphick and Wocjan.\n\nWe also demonstrate that for certain graphs, non-integer values of p$p$ provide sharper lower bounds than existing spectral bounds. As an example, we determine {\\chi }_{q}${\\chi }_{q}$ for the Tilley graph, which cannot be achieved using existing (unweighted) p$p$-energy bounds. Our proof employs a novel synthesis of linear algebra and measure-theoretic tools, which allows us to surpass existing spectral bounds.\n\n  * ### The change of vertex energy when joining trees\n\n2024, Linear Algebra and Its Applications\n\nCitation Excerpt :\n\nThe basics of the theory were developed in [3], where the authors derived fundamental inequalities and gave examples and counterexamples of natural conjectures for the vertex energy. Taking into account the developed theory and in search of an interpretation that would obey intuition and allow us to understand the interaction between local and global energy in graphs, in [2] the authors defined the graph energy game. An important conclusion is that, by considering the vector of vertex energies as the payoff vector of the cooperative game, an interpretation of how the total energy is distributed among the vertices of the graph can be offered.\n\nShow abstract\n\nIn this manuscript we study how the vertex energy of a tree is affected when joined with a bipartite graph. We find an alternating pattern with respect to the coalescence vertex: the energy decreases for vertices located at odd distances and increases for those located at even distances.\n\n  * ### A RESTARTED AND RANDOMIZED ALGORITHM FOR EVALUATING ENERGY OF EXTREMELY LARGE-SCALE GRAPH\n\n2026, Journal of Computational Mathematics\n\n  * ### Bounds on the spread and trace norm of the adjacency matrix of a graph\n\n2026, Quaestiones Mathematicae\n\n  * ### Vertex energy of some integral graphs\n\n2025, International Journal of Mathematics for Industry\n\nView full text\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nConcernin